<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lazica</h1><p class="st-meta">130 CE – 697 CE (approx.) · capital Archaeopolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A western Georgian kingdom on the Black Sea coast, heir of ancient Colchis, over which Byzantium and Persia fought the long Lazic War of the 6th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lazi, a Kartvelian people of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis</a>, rose to dominance on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast in the 2nd century CE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, touring the coast for Emperor Hadrian in the 130s, recorded a Lazi king holding power by Roman appointment, and the Lazi gradually united neighbouring tribes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokalakevi">Archaeopolis</a>, modern Nokalakevi, over a land of forests, river valleys and coastal forts. Lazica was a client of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzath-i">Tzath I</a> was baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 522, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> official. The kingdom controlled trade routes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> vital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> navies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 541 the Lazi invited Persian help, sparking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazic-war">Lazic War</a>; after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gubazes-ii-of-lazica">Gubazes II</a> was murdered by Byzantine officers, a peace of 562 returned Lazica to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. Arab raids in the 7th century weakened it, and its lands passed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-abkhazia">Kingdom of Abkhazia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lazica.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lazica" width="320" height="358"><figcaption>Image: Ercwlff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egrisi-4-5_cc.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>130 CE – 697 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Archaeopolis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tzath I, Gubazes II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Laz (Kartvelian); Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lazica, Egrisi, Lazic Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=414&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lazica" aria-label="Kingdom of Lazica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5703 671 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lazica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6023 927L6019 930M6023 927L5304 942M6023 927L5709 970M6023 927L6357 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6019 930h0M5304 942h0M5709 970h0M6357 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6023 927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6023 927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=414&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lazica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
